From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yzddmr6 <46088090+yzddmr6@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 19:00:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] arm64: ARM_LARGE_MEMORY layout with 4 KiB pages from
 level 0

The k27 kernel links at the iOS small-memory layout, whose kernel map
cannot cover the default 9 GiB GunyahVM macOS guest. Use XNU's own
ARM_LARGE_MEMORY layout instead (41-bit kernel pointers, 64 GiB KC and
globals, 768 GiB heap, the Mac's 47-bit user range), which is also the
layout of Apple's vma2 kernel collection.

Gunyah's stage 1 supports 4 KiB but not 16 KiB translation granules
(ID_AA64MMFR0_EL1.TGran16 = 0), so the large layout runs on XNU's
parameterized four-level tables: T0SZ = T1SZ = 17, roots at level 0
indexed by VA[46:39]. The bootstrap pool holds an L0 root each for the
physical and kernel maps; arm_vm_init resolves its flat L1 accesses
through L0. The small-VA hardware L0 wrapper of the 39-bit user pmap is
kept for the small layout only.
